    "q-129154": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129154",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,3,7)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-2x-3y+6z=16.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-2$ and $y=0$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-3y+6z & =16 \\\\ -2(-2)-3(0)+6z & =16 \\\\ 6z & =12 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-2,0,2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-2,0,2⟩-⟨5,3,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,-3,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-2,-3,6⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-7)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+(-5)\\cdot 6|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(6)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|14+9-30|}{\\sqrt{4+9+36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-7|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
