    "q-129166": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129166",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-2,3,-6)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-x+2y+2z=8.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-2$ and $y=1$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2y+2z & =8 \\\\ -1(-2)+2(1)+2z & =8 \\\\ 2z & =4 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-2,1,2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-2,1,2⟩-⟨-2,3,-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-1,2,2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0-4+16|}{\\sqrt{1+4+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|12|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
